{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was marked by political instability in the Crusader states. Raymond inherited the county at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated. Because of his young age a regency was established with his mother Hodierna playing a key role in governance. The regency lasted until he came of age and took full control of Tripoli. During this time the county faced constant threats from Muslim forces and internal rivalries among the nobility.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III became a central figure in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a close ally of Queen Melisende and later served as regent for the young King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond was appointed regent in 1174 and held the position for several years. His leadership was controversial because of his cautious approach to warfare. He often favored diplomacy over military action which earned him both support and criticism. He was also involved in the complex power struggles between the nobility and the military orders such as the Knights Templar and the Knights Hospitaller. His relationship with Guy of Lusignan was particularly tense and contributed to the division within the kingdom.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the events leading to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few nobles who warned against engaging Saladin in open battle. His advice was ignored and the Crusader army suffered a crushing defeat. After the battle Raymond retreated to Tripoli and tried to organize resistance. He died in 1187 shortly after the fall of Jerusalem. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli which had been one of the most stable Crusader states. Raymond was remembered as a thoughtful but often underestimated leader. He was known for his intelligence his diplomatic skills and his deep commitment to preserving Christian holdings in the Levant despite the odds. His legacy is complex but he remains an important figure in the history of the Crusades.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -3.111314072157256e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0024852838832885027}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -4.768258077092469e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.014164037071168423}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -1.0967194612021558e-05}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.002849211450666189}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.005493544042110443}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.08057871460914612}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0008012421894818544}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.00016735584358684719}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -3.1470757676288486e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.0728830375228426e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.4740912914276123}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0028123611118644476}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.264974000354414e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.5258672647178173e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.001625